{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Module format: WHY this is NOT "commonjs"/"node16", even though a VS
    // Code extension's compiled `main` entry traditionally loads as
    // CommonJS via Node's require() (see package.json's "type": "commonjs",
    // which records that intent for whenever a build step exists — none
    // does yet, see the "buildless this run" note below).
    //
    // This package's one load-bearing production import is
    // `@dimm-city/gutterpress-editor` (packages/editor), which is ESM-only
    // by design (package.json "type": "module") and exposes its surface
    // ONLY via a package.json "exports" map pointing directly at TypeScript
    // source — no "main", no "types" fallback (see packages/editor's own
    // src/index.ts header: "there is no build step yet"). That is the same
    // Bun-first, non-emitting convention packages/cli and packages/editor
    // already use in this monorepo.
    //
    // Empirically verified against this repo's exact pinned `typescript`
    // (5.9.3, see root bun.lock) before choosing this pairing:
    //   - "moduleResolution": "node10"/"node" CANNOT see a package's
    //     "exports" map at all — tsc reports the module's real path but
    //     refuses it: "this result could not be resolved under your
    //     current 'moduleResolution' setting. Consider updating to
    //     'node16', 'nodenext', or 'bundler'" (TS2307).
    //   - "module": "node16" (the only module setting "moduleResolution":
    //     "node16" accepts — TS5110 rejects any other pairing) DOES see
    //     the export, but then correctly refuses it for a different
    //     reason: a CommonJS-mode file statically importing an ESM-only
    //     package cannot become a `require()` call (TS1479 — "the
    //     referenced file is an ECMAScript module and cannot be imported
    //     with 'require'. Consider writing a dynamic 'import(...)' call
    //     instead").
    //   - "moduleResolution": "bundler" flatly REJECTS "module": "commonjs"
    //     under this exact TypeScript version (TS5095 — "'bundler' can
    //     only be used when 'module' is set to 'preserve' or to 'es2015'
    //     or later").
    // "module": "preserve" + "moduleResolution": "bundler" is the only
    // combination that typechecks the real dependency edge this run exists
    // to prove (SFE-P1a: "the import is the point"), and it matches
    // packages/cli's and packages/editor's own tsconfig pairing exactly.
    //
    // This is a TYPECHECK-TIME choice only — this run adds no build/emit
    // step (Lane C's deliverable is `typecheck`/`test` scripts only;
    // "keep it buildless for this run"), so nothing here governs any
    // compiled output yet. Reconciling "main"'s eventual CommonJS
    // extension-host load with `@dimm-city/gutterpress-editor` being
    // ESM-only (e.g. a build step that bundles the dependency, a dynamic
    // `import()` indirection, or shipping this extension as an ESM VS Code
    // extension — VS Code has supported those since ~1.90) is deferred to
    // the run that adds a real build step and @vscode/test-electron
    // (recorded gap, alongside the already-noted @vscode/test-electron
    // deferral — see src/extension.ts's header).
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "moduleDetection": "force",

    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    // The extension host is a Node process (D9: "VS Code host owns...
    // file and workspace events"), so Node builtins are permitted in this
    // package — unlike packages/editor (D4: framework-free, browser-safe,
    // "no ... node:fs ... imports"). src/provider.ts uses "node:crypto"
    // for its webview CSP nonce.
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": false
  },
  // Mirrors packages/editor/tsconfig.json: tests/ is part of the strict
  // typecheck program (this is a brand-new package with no other layer
  // that would otherwise catch a type error in a test file, and the run's
  // gate gives `typecheck` and `test` separate, equally load-bearing
  // commands).
  "include": ["src", "tests"]
}
